# a-list-that-is-mixed — plan 31 follow-up, and what the composition costs.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The same base as `a-list-of-one-symbol.keys` with the `schedule`
# line REMOVED**, so it plays the schedule every `WaveState` is born
# with — which since 2026-08-29 carries the design's composition from
# `examples/waves.json` § parts, mirrored by `waves.loft::
# wave_parts_default` and gated against the file part for part.
#
# ⚠ So the pair is one line apart and the line is *author a flat list*
# against *take the shipped one*.  Same ground, wall, tower, crew,
# spawn, poke and ticks.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The SIZES are identical** — 5 / 8 / 12 / 20 / 30 / 50 / 80 in
# both — so what moves the clock is the MIX and can be nothing else.
# That is `@M020`'s reading applied on purpose: a wave is worth its
# front class PLUS what the front cannot cover, so a composition at
# constant size is the change worth measuring.
#
# ⚠ Waves 0 and 1 carry no part in either file: the opening five and
# eight are the player's first sight of a robot and `@X246`'s neutral
# landing zone wants them legible.  The mix starts at wave 2.

echo a list that is mixed: the same counts, composed

do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 20 0
marker 20 0 spawn 3
count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -4 -3 22 -3
drag -4 -2 22 -2
drag -4 -1 22 -1
drag -4 0 22 0
drag -4 1 22 1
drag -4 2 22 2
drag -4 3 22 3

# A wall to chew, and a tower behind it — the difference between the
# classes is a WALL-DAMAGE rate and a SPEED, so a base with nothing to
# chew could not tell them apart at all.
# ⚠ A GATE at (6, 0) — the wall is painted around it — so the player can
# drive out to poke the marker and back.  `plans/12` B7 measured that a
# wall with a gate in it buys ONE tick over a sealed one, and it is
# identical in both halves of this pair either way.
palette 9
click 6 -3
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
click 6 3

# ⚠⚠ **Three towers and two crew, and the base needs every one of
# them**: the design's mix starts at WAVE 2, so a base that cannot reach
# wave 2 cannot tell the two halves of this pair apart at all.  The
# first version of this fixture had one tower, fell at 175 having sent
# two waves, and read **175 against 175** — a flat pair measuring a base
# that died before the content it was measuring arrived.
do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
click 3 -2
marker 3 -2 tower
click 1 0
marker 1 0 tower
click 3 2
marker 3 2 tower
do toggle_mode
top 3 -2 on
top 1 0 on
top 3 2 on

# ⚠ The crew repair the towers between waves — `plans/17` T1 measured
# that without upkeep more towers buy NOTHING (3, 5 and 7 towers give
# 321, 319 and 317 ticks and four waves each).
crew 2 -1
crew 2 1
park 0 0

# ⚠⚠ **No `schedule` line — that is the whole difference.**  The state
# is born with the design's list already composed.

# ── The poke ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ The wave list does not start until the player drives out and touches
# a spawn marker 12+ hexes from the core (`WAVE_1_PROVOCATION_HEXES`),
# so both files start their clock the same way and at the same tick.
waves 0 0
drive 20 0
tick 14
vehicle 20 0
waves 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 14
vehicle 0 0


# ── ⚠⚠ THE READING, AND THE SCOUTS ARE THE WHOLE OF IT ──────────
#
# **452 flat against 515 composed — the mix makes the base last 63
# ticks LONGER**, which is the opposite of what the raw arithmetic
# says.  On wall damage per second the composed list is mostly
# STRONGER: wave 3 is 24.4 against 20.0, wave 6 is 87.2 against 80.0.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The attribution is measured, not reasoned** (`@M089`).  Swapping
# in only the slow, strong classes reads **464**; swapping in only the
# scouts reads **515**; the full mix reads **515** — identical to
# scouts-alone, so the miners contribute nothing once scouts are there:
#
#     flat, all regular                       452
#     miners + harvesters, no scouts          464   (+12)
#     scouts only                             515   (+63)
#     the shipped composition                 515   (+63)
#
# ⚠⚠ **The mechanism is `@M020` with its sign flipped.**  The siege
# front is the wall's WIDTH, so only about six robots chew at once and
# the rest of a wave is queued behind them — *a wave is worth its front
# class PLUS what the front cannot cover*.  A **scout moves 2.5 hex/s
# against a regular's 1.5 and does 0.1 wall damage against a regular's
# 1.0**, so scouts reach the wall FIRST, take every slot in the front,
# and chew at a TENTH of the rate — while the miners that would do
# triple damage are still walking.  ⚠ **The fast class screens the wall
# from the slow one**, and the wave's own composition is what defends
# the base.
#
# ⚠⚠ **So this composition SOFTENS the authored list by 14 %, and that
# is a content decision nobody has ruled on.**  It is recorded rather
# than tuned away: `ROADMAP.md` § THE SESSION IS THE GAP NOW says the
# wave list is a placeholder for the economy (item 10) and that tuning
# it now is balancing a placeholder.  ⚠ What this pair makes possible is
# the tuning being INFORMED when it happens — *adding a fast weak class
# to a wave makes it weaker*, which no arithmetic over the rates shows.
fall 900
ticks 511 519
